{"conjecture": "Long-running systems ossify because abstraction boundaries, initially chosen for convenience rather than stability, become load-bearing API contracts that couple the propagation of logically unrelated changes. Once a boundary freezes into a public interface, any future modification that conceptually spans multiple boundaries must be decomposed into boundary-shaped increments, forcing engineers to serialize and coordinate intrinsically concurrent design decisions. The system thus becomes harder to change not because any single change was wrong, but because frozen boundaries collectively prevent the reshuffling of responsibilities that ongoing correct evolution requires."}
